1
00:00:00,410 --> 00:00:03,300
Hi. You've learned the process

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,370
of crafting a narrative
about your project,

3
00:00:05,370 --> 00:00:06,959
and you've learned
how to visualize

4
00:00:06,959 --> 00:00:10,065
key data points that help
you to tell that story.

5
00:00:10,065 --> 00:00:12,990
Now it's time to
bring it all together.

6
00:00:12,990 --> 00:00:14,430
In this video, we'll highlight

7
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:17,010
some useful presentation
techniques.

8
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:19,950
Think about one of your
favorite public speakers

9
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:22,485
and why you enjoy their
presentations so much.

10
00:00:22,485 --> 00:00:24,885
Is it their voice,
their knowledge,

11
00:00:24,885 --> 00:00:27,375
or the confidence they
exude while speaking?

12
00:00:27,375 --> 00:00:30,125
Brene Brown is one of
my favorite speakers.

13
00:00:30,125 --> 00:00:32,675
She's widely known
for her 2010 TED Talk,

14
00:00:32,675 --> 00:00:34,190
The Power of Vulnerability,

15
00:00:34,190 --> 00:00:37,895
which received 60
million views worldwide.

16
00:00:37,895 --> 00:00:40,940
Brown didn't begin her
career as a public speaker,

17
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:44,465
but as a research professor
at the University of Houston.

18
00:00:44,465 --> 00:00:47,630
Today, she uses public
speaking as a way to

19
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:51,295
teach executives and leaders
about courage and empathy.

20
00:00:51,295 --> 00:00:53,480
Public speakers spend a lot

21
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:55,085
of time working on their craft.

22
00:00:55,085 --> 00:00:57,440
Speaking to an audience—whether
to inform,

23
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:00,430
entertain, or share—isn't
always easy,

24
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:03,560
and data alone isn't enough to
convince people that you've

25
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:05,120
made the right decisions or that

26
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:07,115
your project was impactful.

27
00:01:07,115 --> 00:01:10,100
An effective presentation
helps convey

28
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:11,390
the important work that you and

29
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:14,035
your team are accomplishing
on your project.

30
00:01:14,035 --> 00:01:15,920
And throughout the project, you'll

31
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:17,480
likely have many opportunities to

32
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:19,850
present—from your
initial kickoff meeting

33
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:21,560
to your weekly status updates

34
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:24,445
to your final project
presentation.

35
00:01:24,445 --> 00:01:26,720
As you begin to craft
your narrative,

36
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:28,205
think about your audience.

37
00:01:28,205 --> 00:01:31,685
Ask yourself: What do I
want my audience to know,

38
00:01:31,685 --> 00:01:34,730
think, or do as a result
of this presentation?

39
00:01:34,730 --> 00:01:36,890
Create your presentation around

40
00:01:36,890 --> 00:01:39,625
the big picture, and
keep it simple.

41
00:01:39,625 --> 00:01:42,110
Now let's discuss three ways to

42
00:01:42,110 --> 00:01:44,375
help you give an
effective presentation.

43
00:01:44,375 --> 00:01:48,775
Those are being precise,
flexible, and memorable.

44
00:01:48,775 --> 00:01:52,130
First, to give an
effective presentation,

45
00:01:52,130 --> 00:01:55,025
you need to be precise
about your key points.

46
00:01:55,025 --> 00:01:57,890
Identify the problem you're
solving for your audience and

47
00:01:57,890 --> 00:02:01,375
remove any content that
dilutes your narrative.

48
00:02:01,375 --> 00:02:03,470
One way I ensure that

49
00:02:03,470 --> 00:02:06,140
my slideshow presentations
are as precise as they can

50
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:08,060
be is by using a technique

51
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:10,550
called "designing
for five seconds."

52
00:02:10,550 --> 00:02:13,220
The idea is that your
audience should be

53
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:15,850
able to understand a slide
within five seconds.

54
00:02:15,850 --> 00:02:19,085
So I keep my presentation
slides simple,

55
00:02:19,085 --> 00:02:22,325
including only the most
relevant data points, to avoid

56
00:02:22,325 --> 00:02:23,630
overloading my audience with

57
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:26,075
text they don't
have time to read.

58
00:02:26,075 --> 00:02:28,505
To give an effective
presentation,

59
00:02:28,505 --> 00:02:30,635
you also need to be flexible.

60
00:02:30,635 --> 00:02:32,660
Flexibility is a huge part

61
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:34,385
of your job as a project manager.

62
00:02:34,385 --> 00:02:36,140
For example, it's possible

63
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:38,000
that a stakeholder
may have to leave

64
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:39,980
your presentation unexpectedly or

65
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:42,325
that other attendees
may arrive late.

66
00:02:42,325 --> 00:02:45,470
Consider the approach
you'd take if you had to

67
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:48,230
shorten your presentation
from an hour to 30 minutes,

68
00:02:48,230 --> 00:02:50,015
or even just five.

69
00:02:50,015 --> 00:02:52,070
Know the most important points

70
00:02:52,070 --> 00:02:53,660
you want to make, and be prepared

71
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:57,330
to share only those points,
should the unexpected occur.

72
00:02:57,330 --> 00:02:59,240
Preparing ahead of time also

73
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,745
helps you be more
flexible when presenting.

74
00:03:01,745 --> 00:03:04,220
Ample preparation
helps you to avoid

75
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:05,300
small mistakes that can

76
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:07,250
potentially distract
from your narrative,

77
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:09,050
like stumbling over sentences or

78
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:11,800
struggling to pull up a
slideshow presentation.

79
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:13,460
To prepare ahead of time,

80
00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:15,170
you might practice delivering

81
00:03:15,170 --> 00:03:17,270
your presentation to
members of your team

82
00:03:17,270 --> 00:03:18,965
and invite them to
offer feedback,

83
00:03:18,965 --> 00:03:21,725
ask questions, or share concerns.

84
00:03:21,725 --> 00:03:24,200
Preparing ahead of time also

85
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:26,315
gives you space to
identify and come up with

86
00:03:26,315 --> 00:03:27,800
answers to the types of questions

87
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,120
your audience might have
about your presentation.

88
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:34,460
It also gives you time to
imagine and prepare for

89
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:36,320
possible objections
your audience might

90
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:38,660
have to the decisions
you want to make.

91
00:03:38,660 --> 00:03:41,105
Lastly, be memorable.

92
00:03:41,105 --> 00:03:43,190
Develop a strategy that helps

93
00:03:43,190 --> 00:03:45,635
make your narrative
unforgettable.

94
00:03:45,635 --> 00:03:48,424
Think back to effective
storytelling.

95
00:03:48,424 --> 00:03:50,330
This is the point
where you want to

96
00:03:50,330 --> 00:03:51,980
tie in your data analysis,

97
00:03:51,980 --> 00:03:54,230
effective visualization, and put

98
00:03:54,230 --> 00:03:55,370
the finishing touches on

99
00:03:55,370 --> 00:03:57,470
your narrative to
bring it all together.

100
00:03:57,470 --> 00:04:00,200
Use stories or
include repetition to

101
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:01,430
help your audience remember

102
00:04:01,430 --> 00:04:03,215
the information moving forward.

103
00:04:03,215 --> 00:04:05,030
Another helpful tip is to be aware

104
00:04:05,030 --> 00:04:07,130
of your body
language when presenting.

105
00:04:07,130 --> 00:04:08,990
Maintain an upright posture

106
00:04:08,990 --> 00:04:11,030
and rest your hands at your side.

107
00:04:11,030 --> 00:04:12,650
When making a point, try

108
00:04:12,650 --> 00:04:15,185
elevating your tone of
voice for emphasis.

109
00:04:15,185 --> 00:04:19,310
Pace yourself by using
intentional pauses and

110
00:04:19,310 --> 00:04:20,960
speak about half
the speed you would

111
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:23,990
normally speak while keeping
your sentences short.

112
00:04:23,990 --> 00:04:26,330
Make eye contact with
your audience and keep

113
00:04:26,330 --> 00:04:29,000
your facial expressions
warm and friendly.

114
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:30,795
Finally, have confidence.

115
00:04:30,795 --> 00:04:32,435
You've done the
research and are well-prepared,

116
00:04:32,435 --> 00:04:35,070
you can set your worries aside.

117
00:04:35,070 --> 00:04:37,720
If you're precise, flexible,

118
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:40,520
and memorable, you'll give
a great presentation.

119
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:43,520
Now that you've analyzed the
data and crafted your story,

120
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:46,480
your job is to present
your findings effectively.

121
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:48,350
Use these tips to deliver

122
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:51,275
a presentation worthy
of a standing ovation.

123
00:04:51,275 --> 00:04:52,985
In the next video,

124
00:04:52,985 --> 00:04:54,620
we'll wrap up and
review what we've

125
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:57,840
recently learned. I'll
meet you over there.
